NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002862 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002863 "sub no_op {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002864 "    # this space intentionally blank"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002865 "}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002866 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002867 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002868 "package XML::SAX::Base::NoHandler;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002869 "$XML::SAX::Base::NoHandler::VERSION = '1.09';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002870 "# we need a fake handler that doesn't implement anything, this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002871 "# simplifies the code a lot (though given the recent changes,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002872 "# it may be better to do without)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002873 "sub new {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002874 "    #warn \"no handler called\\n\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002875 "    return bless {};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002876 "}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002877 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002878 "1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002879 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002880 "__END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002881 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002882 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002883 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002884 "XML::SAX::Base - Base class SAX Drivers and Filters"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002885 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002886 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002887 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002888 "  package MyFilter;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002889 "  use XML::SAX::Base;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002890 "  @ISA = ('XML::SAX::Base');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002891 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002892 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002893 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002894 "This module has a very simple task - to be a base class for PerlSAX"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002895 "drivers and filters. It's default behaviour is to pass the input directly"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002896 "to the output unchanged. It can be useful to use this module as a base class"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002897 "so you don't have to, for example, implement the characters() callback."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002898 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002899 "The main advantages that it provides are easy dispatching of events the right"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002900 "way (ie it takes care for you of checking that the handler has implemented"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002901 "that method, or has defined an AUTOLOAD), and the guarantee that filters"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002902 "will pass along events that they aren't implementing to handlers downstream"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002903 "that might nevertheless be interested in them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002904 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002905 "=head1 WRITING SAX DRIVERS AND FILTERS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002906 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002907 "The Perl Sax API Reference is at L<http://perl-xml.sourceforge.net/perl-sax/>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002908 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002909 "Writing SAX Filters is tremendously easy: all you need to do is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002910 "inherit from this module, and define the events you want to handle. A"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002911 "more detailed explanation can be found at"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002912 "http://www.xml.com/pub/a/2001/10/10/sax-filters.html."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002913 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002914 "Writing Drivers is equally simple. The one thing you need to pay"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002915 "attention to is B<NOT> to call events yourself (this applies to Filters"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002916 "as well). For instance:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002917 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002918 "  package MyFilter;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002919 "  use base qw(XML::SAX::Base);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002920 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002921 "  sub start_element {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002922 "    my $self = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002923 "    my $data = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002924 "    # do something"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002925 "    $self->{Handler}->start_element($data); # BAD"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002926 "  }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002927 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002928 "The above example works well as precisely that: an example. But it has"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002929 "several faults: 1) it doesn't test to see whether the handler defines"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002930 "start_element. Perhaps it doesn't want to see that event, in which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002931 "case you shouldn't throw it (otherwise it'll die). 2) it doesn't check"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002932 "ContentHandler and then Handler (ie it doesn't look to see that the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002933 "user hasn't requested events on a specific handler, and if not on the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002934 "default one), 3) if it did check all that, not only would the code be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002935 "cumbersome (see this module's source to get an idea) but it would also"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002936 "probably have to check for a DocumentHandler (in case this were SAX1)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002937 "and for AUTOLOADs potentially defined in all these packages. As you can"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002938 "tell, that would be fairly painful. Instead of going through that,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002939 "simply remember to use code similar to the following instead:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002940 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002941 "  package MyFilter;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002942 "  use base qw(XML::SAX::Base);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002943 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002944 "  sub start_element {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002945 "    my $self = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002946 "    my $data = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002947 "    # do something to filter"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002948 "    $self->SUPER::start_element($data); # GOOD (and easy) !"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002949 "  }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002950 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002951 "This way, once you've done your job you hand the ball back to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002952 "XML::SAX::Base and it takes care of all those problems for you!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002953 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002954 "Note that the above example doesn't apply to filters only, drivers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002955 "will benefit from the exact same feature."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002956 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002957 "=head1 METHODS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002958 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002959 "A number of methods are defined within this class for the purpose of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002960 "inheritance. Some probably don't need to be overridden (eg parse_file)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002961 "but some clearly should be (eg parse). Options for these methods are"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002962 "described in the PerlSAX2 specification available from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002963 "http://cvs.sourceforge.net/cgi-bin/viewcvs.cgi/~checkout~/perl-xml/libxml-perl/doc/sax-2.0.html?rev=HEAD&content-type=text/html."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002964 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002965 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002966 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002967 "=item * parse"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002968 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002969 "The parse method is the main entry point to parsing documents. Internally"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002970 "the parse method will detect what type of \"thing\" you are parsing, and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002971 "call the appropriate method in your implementation class. Here is the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002972 "mapping table of what is in the Source options (see the Perl SAX 2.0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002973 "specification for the meaning of these values):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002974 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002975 "  Source Contains           parse() calls"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002976 "  ===============           ============="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002977 "  CharacterStream (*)       _parse_characterstream($stream, $options)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002978 "  ByteStream                _parse_bytestream($stream, $options)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002979 "  String                    _parse_string($string, $options)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002980 "  SystemId                  _parse_systemid($string, $options)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002981 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002982 "However note that these methods may not be sensible if your driver class "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002983 "is not for parsing XML. An example might be a DBI driver that generates"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002984 "XML/SAX from a database table. If that is the case, you likely want to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002985 "write your own parse() method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002986 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002987 "Also note that the Source may contain both a PublicId entry, and an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002988 "Encoding entry. To get at these, examine $options->{Source} as passed"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002989 "to your method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002990 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002991 "(*) A CharacterStream is a filehandle that does not need any encoding"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002992 "translation done on it. This is implemented as a regular filehandle"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002993 "and only works under Perl 5.7.2 or higher using PerlIO. To get a single"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002994 "character, or number of characters from it, use the perl core read()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002995 "function. To get a single byte from it (or number of bytes), you can "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002996 "use sysread(). The encoding of the stream should be in the Encoding"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002997 "entry for the Source."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002998 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002999 "=item * parse_file, parse_uri, parse_string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003000 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003001 "These are all convenience variations on parse(), and in fact simply"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003002 "set up the options before calling it. You probably don't need to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003003 "override these."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003004 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003005 "=item * get_options"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003006 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003007 "This is a convenience method to get options in SAX2 style, or more"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003008 "generically either as hashes or as hashrefs (it returns a hashref)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003009 "You will probably want to use this method in your own implementations"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003010 "of parse() and of new()."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003011 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003012 "=item * get_feature, set_feature"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003013 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003014 "These simply get and set features, and throw the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003015 "appropriate exceptions defined in the specification if need be."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003016 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003017 "If your subclass defines features not defined in this one,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003018 "then you should override these methods in such a way that they check for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003019 "your features first, and then call the base class's methods"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003020 "for features not defined by your class. An example would be:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003021 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003022 "  sub get_feature {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003023 "      my $self = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003024 "      my $feat = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003025 "      if (exists $MY_FEATURES{$feat}) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003026 "          # handle the feature in various ways"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003027 "      }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003028 "      else {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003029 "          return $self->SUPER::get_feature($feat);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003030 "      }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003031 "  }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003032 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003033 "Currently this part is unimplemented."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003034 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003035 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003036 "=item * set_handler"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003037 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003038 "This method takes a handler type (Handler, ContentHandler, etc.) and a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003039 "handler object as arguments, and changes the current handler for that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003040 "handler type, while taking care of resetting the internal state that "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003041 "needs to be reset. This allows one to change a handler during parse"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003042 "without running into problems (changing it on the parser object "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003043 "directly will most likely cause trouble)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003044 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003045 "=item * set_document_handler, set_content_handler, set_dtd_handler, set_lexical_handler, set_decl_handler, set_error_handler, set_entity_resolver"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003046 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003047 "These are just simple wrappers around the former method, and take a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003048 "handler object as their argument. Internally they simply call"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003049 "set_handler with the correct arguments."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003051 "=item * get_handler"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003052 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003053 "The inverse of set_handler, this method takes a an optional string containing a handler type (DTDHandler, "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003054 "ContentHandler, etc. 'Handler' is used if no type is passed). It returns a reference to the object that implements"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003055 "that class, or undef if that handler type is not set for the current driver/filter. "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003056 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003057 "=item * get_document_handler, get_content_handler, get_dtd_handler, get_lexical_handler, get_decl_handler, "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003058 "get_error_handler, get_entity_resolver"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003059 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003060 "These are just simple wrappers around the get_handler() method, and take no arguments. Internally "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003061 "they simply call get_handler with the correct handler type name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003062 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003063 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003064 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003065 "It would be rather useless to describe all the methods that this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003066 "module implements here. They are all the methods supported in SAX1 and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003067 "SAX2. In case your memory is a little short, here is a list. The"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003068 "apparent duplicates are there so that both versions of SAX can be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003069 "supported."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003070 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003071 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003072 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003073 "=item * start_document"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003074 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003075 "=item * end_document"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003076 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003077 "=item * start_element"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003078 ""
